f3e1121" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338729/field-2/ma-v1/da571d27153381b6256ab4277a1ea4a0f0aeb693589b21c8f35a0e9c753d3dc8/36e6336849db2f6874650bd3e2f2de2f55814ff4ddd45c9f0c2d0ee469ecd8a4" :answer-field/choices ["q-338729/field-2/ma-v1/da571d27153381b6256ab4277a1ea4a0f0aeb693589b21c8f35a0e9c753d3dc8/0f3ef08b048f237d00165c0a317a78a24923ef4462b93407627614d0037325b8" "q-338729/field-2/ma-v1/da571d27153381b6256ab4277a1ea4a0f0aeb693589b21c8f35a0e9c753d3dc8/2687d391779023c3e28b0feb2ae054fdf2c7c082851e48059668135fa4e5e305" "q-338729/field-2/ma-v1/da571d27153381b6256ab4277a1ea4a0f0aeb693589b21c8f35a0e9c753d3dc8/ba5523a97c188f54532f82b730141550cfe80c7258d324a520bbd20d7ad29f0c" "q-338729/field-2/ma-v1/da571d27153381b6256ab4277a1ea4a0f0aeb693589b21c8f35a0e9c753d3dc8/36e6336849db2f6874650bd3e2f2de2f55814ff4ddd45c9f0c2d0ee469ecd8a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-338729"] :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8\\lambda {y}^{′}+16{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-8\\lambda m+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-4\\lambda )(m-4\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=4\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{4\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda x}+B{e}^{4\\lambda x}+4B\\lambda x{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda }+B{e}^{4\\lambda }+4B\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4A\\lambda +B+4B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+2B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(4A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(4A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+8A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+(4+16\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda  & 1+4\\lambda  \\\\ 1+8\\lambda  & 4(1+4\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+4\\lambda )-(1+4\\lambda )(1+8\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -(1+8\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -1-8\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )(8\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-338729/field-1/ma-v1/5b50e26cba1d6bce93f49b533425c5e2b15468f951754a8a17b4605cb5432ae7" "q-338729/field-2/ma-v1/da571d27153381b6256ab4277a1ea4a0f0aeb693589b21c8f35a0e9c753d3dc8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-338853/field-1/ef0d9d6c0fb82916854d020568f97fb09f2cbdf50227cd355cac925271fc81d6" :answer/id #uuid "8f544337-0e9f-5861-a470-ac7d7749ebd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338853/field-1/6c5183b804165499a69b40f6090f66f1a122a0207e8bac4bf82d49bc4ef63202" :answer/id #uuid "46914119-61c0-55e3-b0b1-9ba8f00a5b4e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338853/field-1/bf7083ab0303a7c61903f56ddfc070744cca53d8bf4b28d9252c4e0dfd6c77ea" :answer/id #uuid "5d30216a-c2b4-5a27-aa70-4e3cc4f526ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-\\frac{7}{18}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338853/field-1/1c031025bcffa2d4ec623a5bad9f0e8f18d281fd1a55449902d3de01850db8cd" :answer/id #uuid "91ba17bf-c8a7-50d8-addb-789f7e63e8ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{7}{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338853/field-1/2f817eb15f994caf46175717f58bb0f51c9b451dbd74a973fe420ad3a98f96f2" :answer/id #uuid "edbe8ed7-d3d8-57e9-9324-55582f348a31" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338853/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "468ce62f-1113-5665-85a3-5c884c570372" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338853/field-1/bf7083ab0303a7c61903f56ddfc070744cca53d8bf4b28d9252c4e0dfd6c77ea" :answer-field/choices ["q-338853/field-1/ef0d9d6c0fb82916854d020568f97fb09f2cbdf50227cd355cac925271fc81d6" "q-338853/field-1/6c5183b804165499a69b40f6090f66f1a122a0207e8bac4bf82d49bc4ef63202" "q-338853/field-1/bf7083ab0303a7c61903f56ddfc070744cca53d8bf4b28d9252c4e0dfd6c77ea" "q-338853/field-1/1c031025bcffa2d4ec623a5bad9f0e8f18d281fd1a55449902d3de01850db8cd" "q-338853/field-1/2f817eb15f994caf46175717f58bb0f51c9b451dbd74a973fe420ad3a98f96f2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338853/field-2/9b4691457d839ab9262937dfb9708b845537762311d94ff190d3287b567c45da" :answer/id #uuid "c0670a1b-b4c8-534a-9097-3801f5691eef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-7x/6}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338853/field-2/6c540d83b3990efd8f637613dc939b4e11141215538eca200e7222181d6475f8" :answer/id #uuid "402b4be1-fdcb-5321-95f9-d09764532176" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338853/field-2/cfa7e1950893a8c0248baa13d742ac0239a9c66dbf7d666f735111417852376e" :answer/id #uuid "fd8e4b95-91b7-541d-b14a-1f2fb08145f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-7x/3}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338853/field-2/0a9f45c77a163a91e3677d3be9ea44cf70dad8ef14029c4c6424a95ce438e58c" :answer/id #uuid "2777bc12-e5ee-5651-97a1-9f500bcf66d4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{7x/6}$ and $Cx{e}^{-7x/6}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338853/field-2/2bba317cd311a6c4650992163cfe4c5274233afe30ad3cf6223a01396eb17f62" :answer/id #uuid "9caccf85-2557-57cc-9fef-93abdcc03be0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-x}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338853/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "17ffc179-bde8-5331-9899-6419a9455487" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338853/field-2/9b4691457d839ab9262937dfb9708b845537762311d94ff190d3287b567c45da" :answer-field/choices ["q-338853/field-2/9b4691457d839ab9262937dfb9708b845537762311d94ff190d3287b567c45da" "q-338853/field-2/6c540d83b3990efd8f637613dc939b4e11141215538eca200e7222181d6475f8" "q-338853/field-2/cfa7e1950893a8c0248baa13d742ac0239a9c66dbf7d666f735111417852376e" "q-338853/field-2/0a9f45c77a163a91e3677d3be9ea44cf70dad8ef14029c4c6424a95ce438e58c" "q-338853/field-2/2bba317cd311a6c4650992163cfe4c5274233afe30ad3cf6223a01396eb17f62"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338853" :question/id #uuid "4fbda52c-e557-5901-ad9a-c542b15bd0a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-338853" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-6\\lambda {y}^{′}+9{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,2y(2)+3{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $2y(2)+3{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-6\\lambda m+9{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-3\\lambda )(m-3\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=3\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{3\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{3\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{3\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{3\\lambda x}+3B\\lambda x{e}^{3\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $2y(2)+3{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2B{e}^{6\\lambda })+3(B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ 4B{e}^{6\\lambda }+3B{e}^{6\\lambda }+18B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{6\\lambda }(7+18\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{6\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{7}{18}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{3\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-7x/6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338853/field-1" "q-338853/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338853"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338904/field-1/94cd35bb03b241357ba7106324ab9ff6233c2d13aabc3bfe2314266b14321d9c" :answer/id #uuid "02ebf14a-62f8-59ba-9dae-75782a9881c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{12}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338904/field-1/9ac953b12593186f038c615aa3a2aaab819c1eb253a4d9d1d96ab1831309de81" :answer/id #uuid "53048c9f-4a12-5e1d-8602-d7946b83a6ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338904/field-1/188a582a6ada47ace951977306425e561e567d438bc272b1e5f974a0fa800cac" :answer/id #uuid "e408e244-93a5-5153-ae8f-5a34db29684a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{6}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338904/field-1/2e1c11e16095c15acb224849863a7396581ffabf74e966263b1966b16249a920" :answer/id #uuid "0710e9b5-45f4-555d-9c51-8526e3539b59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338904/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b8bfa53-bacb-592a-9570-c0b42e4de6d3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338904/field-1/94cd35bb03b241357ba7106324ab9ff6233c2d13aabc3bfe2314266b14321d9c" :answer-field/choices ["q-338904/field-1/94cd35bb03b241357ba7106324ab9ff6233c2d13aabc3bfe2314266b14321d9c" "q-338904/field-1/9ac953b12593186f038c615aa3a2aaab819c1eb253a4d9d1d96ab1831309de81" "q-338904/field-1/188a582a6ada47ace951977306425e561e567d438bc272b1e5f974a0fa800cac" "q-338904/field-1/2e1c11e16095c15acb224849863a7396581ffabf74e966263b1966b16249a920"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338904/field-2/b431ce6c675c63d60e18fc880c8b0bd6de429fd8c3c71cab7850235ffba9fb57" :answer/id #uuid "9887355e-f22b-5da2-aef0-23f7686a150b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338904/field-2/6183d51c7351d66aaaf262e2f93b0e1245d44a44fd1f80e78eba9701368bf135" :answer/id #uuid "b1e25f0b-ebad-5ddd-8d45-e914cde44dfe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338904/field-2/57dc4fcf908555b236e891859627473c7d5f092d37125b9bf5510dc5ed4f6890" :answer/id #uuid "c1809112-54a8-5c1e-8d90-53afba6039ba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338904/field-2/91e11865700142e6e76ff0fb578ee4c63e7fe679b93945f02e6a42455dfd4df8" :answer/id #uuid "943988ef-b9fc-5281-9019-734b52ce21e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x-1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338904/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c9dc26a-3f27-5dc0-92f2-273dd7b13cf0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338904/field-2/b431ce6c675c63d60e18fc880c8b0bd6de429fd8c3c71cab7850235ffba9fb57" :answer-field/choices ["q-338904/field-2/b431ce6c675c63d60e18fc880c8b0bd6de429fd8c3c71cab7850235ffba9fb57" "q-338904/field-2/6183d51c7351d66aaaf262e2f93b0e1245d44a44fd1f80e78eba9701368bf135" "q-338904/field-2/57dc4fcf908555b236e891859627473c7d5f092d37125b9bf5510dc5ed4f6890" "q-338904/field-2/91e11865700142e6e76ff0fb578ee4c63e7fe679b93945f02e6a42455dfd4df8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338904" :question/id #uuid "a75e4f01-4a1b-5592-b071-be5563b560e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-338904" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-12\\lambda {y}^{′}+36{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12\\lambda m+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-6\\lambda )(m-6\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=6\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{6\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{6\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda x}+B{e}^{6\\lambda x}+6B\\lambda x{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (6\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+2(6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(6A\\lambda +B+6B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+12A\\lambda +2B+12B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(6\\lambda )A+B=0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+12\\lambda  & 3+12\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda (3+12\\lambda )-(1)(1+12\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 18\\lambda +72{\\lambda }^{2}-1-12\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 72{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (12\\lambda -1)(6\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-338904/field-1" "q-338904/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338904"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338905/field-1/4f808f75630a53db2665