uuid "82f414f1-5879-572f-839e-fb0e4582d0d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338905/field-2/36e6336849db2f6874650bd3e2f2de2f55814ff4ddd45c9f0c2d0ee469ecd8a4" :answer/id #uuid "eaf3e695-aff2-55cb-950c-697c0f43e6d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338905/field-2/da7a3382a02d6516cb17691d99cc8a634759adabe00d6aa9160109dbb01e5e5d" :answer/id #uuid "28672692-f324-5ee4-8765-0bac338463d0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338905/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "deedf306-a48d-5fd0-a7ea-825b8d69d73c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338905/field-2/36e6336849db2f6874650bd3e2f2de2f55814ff4ddd45c9f0c2d0ee469ecd8a4" :answer-field/choices ["q-338905/field-2/b217ad4719cd8fe439322a5e97bf30493d4b8d7ad32899e7552cada11a2c63b9" "q-338905/field-2/6183d51c7351d66aaaf262e2f93b0e1245d44a44fd1f80e78eba9701368bf135" "q-338905/field-2/36e6336849db2f6874650bd3e2f2de2f55814ff4ddd45c9f0c2d0ee469ecd8a4" "q-338905/field-2/da7a3382a02d6516cb17691d99cc8a634759adabe00d6aa9160109dbb01e5e5d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338905" :question/id #uuid "e67017cf-eb62-5988-9350-d0872682f901" :question/math-academy-id "q-338905" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-20\\lambda {y}^{′}+100{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-20\\lambda m+100{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-10\\lambda )(m-10\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=10\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{10\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{10\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{10\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =10A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda x}+B{e}^{10\\lambda x}+10B\\lambda x{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }+B{e}^{10\\lambda }+10B\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 10A\\lambda +B+10B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{20\\lambda }+2B{e}^{20\\lambda }+2(10A\\lambda {e}^{20\\lambda }+B{e}^{20\\lambda }+20B\\lambda {e}^{20\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(10A\\lambda +B+20B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+20A\\lambda +2B+40B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+(4+40\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+4(1+10\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+4(1+10\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}10\\lambda  & 1+10\\lambda  \\\\ 1+20\\lambda  & 4(1+10\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+10\\lambda )-(1+10\\lambda )(1+20\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )[40\\lambda -(1+20\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )[40\\lambda -1-20\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )(20\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{10}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{20}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{10}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{20},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-338905/field-1" "q-338905/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338905"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338900/field-1/bdfe7a89c370d2103ee02a3da42ed2f9ea09084e6a3801a2ef2af410ff4a2048" :answer/id #uuid "f03a4ae5-96aa-55cc-9ed5-b8e246db8bfb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{8}$ and $\\frac{1}{16}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338900/field-1/254d33a78763d0dc35398db5a0e5f5ca5ae492573d139278698ffc1be8ae5e23" :answer/id #uuid "2f7b67d9-d212-5aed-8b91-5cc27baefc0d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{16}$ and $\\frac{1}{8}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338900/field-1/bbe40b4f02ff56916962d836dd23a137fdf83f2c3c9873ef176594962f2a220e" :answer/id #uuid "d6560db5-5ee8-59cd-a56e-ae19633ffb7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338900/field-1/d7b2b49b6b0df526765c5235be222e91c4033ba6ad43221da72cb66799caed0c" :answer/id #uuid "25634c73-2313-5b4d-a2ea-762153017936" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338900/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "98aa0f9d-989c-5234-b1e3-1d00886a9525" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338900/field-1/bdfe7a89c370d2103ee02a3da42ed2f9ea09084e6a3801a2ef2af410ff4a2048" :answer-field/choices ["q-338900/field-1/bdfe7a89c370d2103ee02a3da42ed2f9ea09084e6a3801a2ef2af410ff4a2048" "q-338900/field-1/254d33a78763d0dc35398db5a0e5f5ca5ae492573d139278698ffc1be8ae5e23" "q-338900/field-1/bbe40b4f02ff56916962d836dd23a137fdf83f2c3c9873ef176594962f2a220e" "q-338900/field-1/d7b2b49b6b0df526765c5235be222e91c4033ba6ad43221da72cb66799caed0c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338900/field-2/b431ce6c675c63d60e18fc880c8b0bd6de429fd8c3c71cab7850235ffba9fb57" :answer/id #uuid "7268cbe5-27b3-5b41-989e-517375f9fe93" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338900/field-2/57dc4fcf908555b236e891859627473c7d5f092d37125b9bf5510dc5ed4f6890" :answer/id #uuid "8314215f-d6f4-57f8-bc53-ad2b4b13d3f7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338900/field-2/91e11865700142e6e76ff0fb578ee4c63e7fe679b93945f02e6a42455dfd4df8" :answer/id #uuid "f2b59f7f-0b67-575b-84c9-c639e18b88c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x-1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338900/field-2/6183d51c7351d66aaaf262e2f93b0e1245d44a44fd1f80e78eba9701368bf135" :answer/id #uuid "30f03516-51d0-5e46-aac3-94bf6b5bdbe1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338900/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef48e50-bb94-517c-8741-e6b3a64b9b36" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338900/field-2/b431ce6c675c63d60e18fc880c8b0bd6de429fd8c3c71cab7850235ffba9fb57" :answer-field/choices ["q-338900/field-2/b431ce6c675c63d60e18fc880c8b0bd6de429fd8c3c71cab7850235ffba9fb57" "q-338900/field-2/57dc4fcf908555b236e891859627473c7d5f092d37125b9bf5510dc5ed4f6890" "q-338900/field-2/91e11865700142e6e76ff0fb578ee4c63e7fe679b93945f02e6a42455dfd4df8" "q-338900/field-2/6183d51c7351d66aaaf262e2f93b0e1245d44a44fd1f80e78eba9701368bf135"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338900" :question/id #uuid "46ccc891-cde1-5fd1-bac2-5a6521022f08" :question/math-academy-id "q-338900" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16\\lambda {y}^{′}+64{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16\\lambda m+64{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-8\\lambda )(m-8\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=8\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{8\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{8\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{8\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda x}+B{e}^{8\\lambda x}+8B\\lambda x{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (8\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(8A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+16A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(8\\lambda )A+B=0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+16\\lambda  & 3+16\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda (3+16\\lambda )-(1)(1+16\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 24\\lambda +128{\\lambda }^{2}-1-16\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 128{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (16\\lambda -1)(8\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-338900/field-1" "q-338900/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338900"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338920/field-1/714d0fe7e3d34149c1d5ac0f9e1f2ff61e4e53d1f13c007323c2617f