68a129ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338920/field-2/80f1cc213bbe29841deebee48dc146b3a0dda404a9e597477e3e70e79474ee42" :answer/id #uuid "65dc78d1-78c5-5a8f-9dfa-cfcde797c39d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338920/field-2/e973902cdf79d4d4b1f4d9644d87cf1496a07dfb91f08ac929b396ed21d289d4" :answer/id #uuid "3d9e7ca4-8089-5cec-9e29-c47350e65bb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338920/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "5302b740-763b-5adc-807b-db12f2cd7328" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338920/field-2/80f1cc213bbe29841deebee48dc146b3a0dda404a9e597477e3e70e79474ee42" :answer-field/choices ["q-338920/field-2/13c929b140c82c8e5245cf5cdaa9f19c4e98f1213fcf16d3ddab4891e6fec11a" "q-338920/field-2/0e666fb0244128d739d9a23d79299d604dbc099975e7f51051c1901935e33b28" "q-338920/field-2/80f1cc213bbe29841deebee48dc146b3a0dda404a9e597477e3e70e79474ee42" "q-338920/field-2/e973902cdf79d4d4b1f4d9644d87cf1496a07dfb91f08ac929b396ed21d289d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338920" :question/id #uuid "55c03286-efa5-5f72-9411-1febab9b4b0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338920" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)=y(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(\\pi )=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n4\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{16}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-338920/field-1" "q-338920/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338920"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164749 :answer-field/correct 17592186164753 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164742 :answer-field/correct 17592186164747 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-338667"] :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+4{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 2\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n2\\lambda =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-338916/field-1/74f2ef2b12ff95c557b86c2965d34b1cdc2cdcb101335b7192d39a423b9d0393" :answer/id #uuid "f0a923df-d100-5ecf-b6d4-8df33f5082db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{100}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338916/field-1/21cd4eff586904560859108d7e7bbf177df7abff7cf6fbfd9de193499bf62c19" :answer/id #uuid "7e4f3869-feae-5b56-9d43-69fd273dc9ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338916/field-1/bb266a05fb31b859b6c15d405f937e78623c34a47e1d7a19c176bf1ae4ee46bc" :answer/id #uuid "7c99f66a-fea1-5385-8ca8-bfcff6747677" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{80}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338916/field-1/3b1df184838e2a080adc8097a8b4bee5984b778708dfa1e6bea3eb8db04f9370" :answer/id #uuid "0f250e22-0d82-5f1f-aa6b-b21875b8b56d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{400}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338916/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f67dead1-ebea-57fb-9d98-cd79285444e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338916/field-1/3b1df184838e2a080adc8097a8b4bee5984b778708dfa1e6bea3eb8db04f9370" :answer-field/choices ["q-338916/field-1/74f2ef2b12ff95c557b86c2965d34b1cdc2cdcb101335b7192d39a423b9d0393" "q-338916/field-1/21cd4eff586904560859108d7e7bbf177df7abff7cf6fbfd9de193499bf62c19" "q-338916/field-1/bb266a05fb31b859b6c15d405f937e78623c34a47e1d7a19c176bf1ae4ee46bc" "q-338916/field-1/3b1df184838e2a080adc8097a8b4bee5984b778708dfa1e6bea3eb8db04f9370"] :db/ensure [:a