0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+25\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 5\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{25}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-338921/field-1" "q-338921/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338921"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338915/field-1/e4e81a0cd42fcde8452511542f3b430e29868ef996625412a18662bd16d71714" :answer/id #uuid "612980ed-947b-5df8-8b15-be575a3ec2f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338915/field-1/43c7e14933dad1be4b705dccd52ba5a0ddf75fdfebed2c70f88fb12444e5d683" :answer/id #uuid "23fd2cbd-6d53-5354-8027-16362316797b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{144}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338915/field-1/526b734532838e9ae282650daeccfc09771d11354aafaef22f69219c3361e37f" :answer/id #uuid "3182a8cd-b165-5e56-82af-af6dadc64769" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338915/field-1/5fe9fcb90339cd88991ee36a3c4080671511416f9f7e973c86f131aa7fc9b866" :answer/id #uuid "d6a12f4f-0483-5a03-8fe9-f0752ce2fc32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{144}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338915/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e0509504-292b-5654-bc80-25b98a9e65d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338915/field-1/43c7e14933dad1be4b705dccd52ba5a0ddf75fdfebed2c70f88fb12444e5d683" :answer-field/choices ["q-338915/field-1/e4e81a0cd42fcde8452511542f3b430e29868ef996625412a18662bd16d71714" "q-338915/field-1/43c7e14933dad1be4b705dccd52ba5a0ddf75fdfebed2c70f88fb12444e5d683" "q-338915/field-1/526b734532838e9ae282650daeccfc09771d11354aafaef22f69219c3361e37f" "q-338915/field-1/5fe9fcb90339cd88991ee36a3c4080671511416f9f7e973c86f131aa7fc9b866"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338915/field-2/274939b1955603718b5c9618f95b6c02ce9ebb759cd5a247bac3e95a16b4041d" :answer/id #uuid "cb08637a-3507-502f-bb35-3a143a0a8c7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338915/field-2/0e666fb0244128d739d9a23d79299d604dbc099975e7f51051c1901935e33b28" :answer/id #uuid "3d8c5d06-5e17-5a98-8449-203de18bb4bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338915/field-2/e973902cdf79d4d4b1f4d9644d87cf1496a07dfb91f08ac929b396ed21d289d4" :answer/id #uuid "a4ed5428-3d25-525f-9f95-304fc2f04acd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338915/field-2/9f7690f296d539f2232a1b5385e105a3c6766d87aaa07ee0b421b884e67ef9a8" :answer/id #uuid "374fa734-7446-5bd1-bf71-64c583bcb57f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338915/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "ace2a4c4-d72b-5b81-a42a-7c78257a08fc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338915/field-2/274939b1955603718b5c9618f95b6c02ce9ebb759cd5a247bac3e95a16b4041d" :answer-field/choices ["q-338915/field-2/274939b1955603718b5c9618f95b6c02ce9ebb759cd5a247bac3e95a16b4041d" "q-338915/field-2/0e666fb0244128d739d9a23d79299d604dbc099975e7f51051c1901935e33b28" "q-338915/field-2/e973902cdf79d4d4b1f4d9644d87cf1496a07dfb91f08ac929b396ed21d289d4" "q-338915/field-2/9f7690f296d539f2232a1b5385e105a3c6766d87aaa07ee0b421b884e67ef9a8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338915" :question/id #uuid "1ea62eee-bb57-5794-b427-82156c628984" :question/math-academy-id "q-338915" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in {N}_{0}.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 3\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n3B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n6\\sqrt{\\lambda }=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi =\\frac{(1+2n)\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in {N}_{0}.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{144}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-338915/field-1" "q-338915/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338915"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21808"] :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0, \\quad y'(1) = 0, \\quad y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0, \\quad 1 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad \\lambda \\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda = 0$ and $\\lambda \\neq 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda = 0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y'(1) =0$ and $y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0$ yields the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda = 0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\neq 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-18\\lambda m+81{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-9\\lambda )(m-9\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m = 9\\lambda.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{9\\lambda x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = xe^{9\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{9\\lambda x} + Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =9A\\lambda {e}^{9\\lambda x}+B{e}^{9\\lambda x}+9B\\lambda x{e}^{9\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y'(1) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9A\\lambda {e}^{9\\lambda }+B{e}^{9\\lambda }+9B\\lambda {e}^{9\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 9A\\lambda +B+9B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (9\\lambda )A+(1+9\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{18\\lambda }+2B{e}^{18\\lambda }+2(9A\\lambda {e}^{18\\lambda }+B{e}^{18\\lambda }+18B\\lambda {e}^{18\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(9A\\lambda +B+18B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+18A\\lambda +2B+36B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+18\\lambda )A+(4+36\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+18\\lambda )A+4(1+9\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(9\\lambda )A+(1+9\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+18\\lambda )A+4(1+9\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/assets/29df2dce71db62482d1664cc2f4033dce94574fb1903d80828f4f1d367f8c9a9.png)\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+9\\lambda )-(1+9\\lambda )(1+18\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+9\\lambda )[36\\lambda -(1+18\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+9\\lambda )[36\\lambda -1-18\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+9\\lambda )(18\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 = -\\dfrac19  \\textrm{ and } \\lambda_2 = \\dfrac1{18}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider $\\lambda_1 = -\\dfrac19.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting $\\lambda_1$ into\n\n$$\n(9\\lambda)A  + (1 + 9\\lambda)B =0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A  + \\left(1 -1\\right)B =0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A = 0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = Bxe^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 = \\dfrac1{18},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting $\\lambda_2$ into\n\n$$\n(9\\lambda)A  + (1 + 9\\lambda)B =0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac12A + \\dfrac32 B = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad A = -3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C = B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively, are $Bxe^{-x} \\textrm{ and } Ce^{x/2}(x-3).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21806"] :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + 9\\lambda y = 0, \\qquad y(0) = y(\\pi) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi, \\quad \\lambda \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda = 0$ and $\\lambda > 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda = 0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0) = y(\\pi) = 0$ yields the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda = 0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda > 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m = \\pm 3\\textrm i\\sqrt{\\lambda} .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda})\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}) + B\\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\cos (0) + B\\sin (0) = 0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A = 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny = B\\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\sin (3\\pi\\sqrt{\\lambda}) = 0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n3\\pi\\sqrt{\\lambda} = n\\pi\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in \\mathbb N.$ This gives the set of eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_n = \\dfrac{n^2}{9}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\ny_n(x) = B_n \\sin(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}), \\qquad B_n \\in\\mathbb R.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
